-- Pair: product type (two-element tuple), with the canonical EQUALITY and the
-- canonical ORDER of a product — both componentwise, both provided by `Pair` itself,
-- each CONDITIONED on exactly the strength its components have.
--
-- ── FOUR PROVISIONS, FOUR CONDITIONS (058 §3.8, WI-869) ─────────────────────────
--
-- A provision's `:- goals` tail scopes its conditions to THAT provision. The four
-- clauses below are two towers, each at two strengths, and they are independent:
-- `Pair[Float, Int64]` has `PartialEq` and `PartialOrd` (Float provides both) and
-- has neither `Eq` nor `Ord` (Float provides neither, NaN being neither
-- self-equal nor ordered), while `Pair[Int64, String]` has all four.
--
-- WHY IT CANNOT BE A SORT-LEVEL `requires` CHAIN — measured twice, both directions.
-- A sort's chain is shared by every provision it makes AND threaded WHOLESALE at
-- every dispatch through the carrier. With an `Eq` chain `Pair[A = Float, B = Int64]`
-- was a LOAD ERROR (WI-835's use-site check refuses a `NonEq` carrier at a parameter
-- whose sort `requires Eq`) — `Pair` stopped being a general PRODUCT. With an
-- `Ord` chain `PartialEq.eq(pair(fst: 1.5, snd: 1), …)` became the load error
-- *"PartialEq.eq.dispatch: … no impl matches"* — an ORDERING requirement broke
-- EQUALITY. So the chain had to carry the WEAKEST condition, and every stronger
-- provision then over-claimed. That is what the per-clause form removes.
--
-- THE CONDITIONS ARE ALSO THE EVIDENCE, exactly as a sort-level `requires` is: the
-- `eq` body dispatches its component compares through the `PartialEq` provision's
-- slots and `compare` through the `Ord` provision's. A slot belonging to a
-- SIBLING provision is present but unfilled at a dispatch that did not earn it, and
-- reading one is a loud refusal rather than a silent wrong answer.
--
-- ── ONE OPERATION FOR THE WHOLE ORDERING SURFACE (WI-876) ───────────────────────
--
-- `compare` carries the lexicographic logic and NOTHING else is written here:
-- `gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` come from `PartialOrd`'s default bodies and `max`/`min` from
-- `Ord`'s. Until WI-876 that was impossible — the eval builtins for those six sat
-- on the SPEC ops and compared host SCALARS only, and a builtin on a spec op shadows
-- every carrier, so ordering a `Pair` cost SEVEN operations where one would do.
-- Host implementations are now keyed PER CARRIER (`operation_map` in a binding
-- block), so a structural `Ord` carrier needs `compare` ALONE.
--
-- Equality needs no per-carrier boilerplate for the analogous reason: `PartialEq.eq`
-- ALREADY dispatched to a carrier's own `eq` (`semantic_equal`). It was the ONE
-- builtin family that did, and that asymmetry is what WI-876 removed.
--
-- ── WHAT SHIPPING THE CANONICAL ORDER COSTS ─────────────────────────────────────
--
-- `Pair` is now a CONCRETE `Ord` provider, so a program declaring a RIVAL pair
-- ordering makes a bracket-less `Ord.compare` on a pair a three-way tie naming
-- this provision too, and `[Ord = Pair]` is refused (058 §3.5 check 3 — a
-- concrete provider is not nameable; WI-861's rung 2a is what would close that).
-- Taken knowingly: the lexicographic order IS a pair's identity, as in every
-- neighbouring language (Haskell `Ord (a, b)`, Rust `Ord for (A, B)`, Python tuples,
-- `std::pair`). Pinned in `wi858_pair_orderings_test`.
--
-- TWO MEASURED LIMITS THAT ARE NOT THIS FILE'S DEFECTS.
--
-- (1) `Set[T = Pair[Float, Int64]]` still LOADS although `Eq[Pair[Float, Int64]]`
-- does not hold. Not an over-claim any more — the provision is conditioned and the
-- goal genuinely fails — but no POSITIVE use-site check for `requires Eq` exists:
-- the only refusal at a written type site is the `NonEq` one, and MEASURED, a key
-- providing nothing at all is accepted too. The remaining halves are the composite
-- `NonEq` derivation `eq.anthill` calls a follow-up, and a positive use-site
-- discharge.
--
-- (2) A NESTED pair whose FIRST component is a `Pair` and whose second is a primitive
-- (`eq(pair(fst: pair(fst: 1, snd: 2), snd: 7), …)`) dies at eval, the second
-- component's compare dispatching to `Pair.eq`. PRE-EXISTING — reproduced on a purely
-- local carrier with no prelude involvement, and `A = Int64, B = <carrier>` passes —
-- but it is this file that makes it reachable from the standard library. WI-871, and
-- pinned in `wi858_pair_orderings_test` so it is not discovered twice.
enum anthill.prelude.Pair
  import anthill.prelude.{Bool, Int64, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, WeakOrd, Ord}

  sort A = ?
  sort B = ?
  entity pair(fst: A, snd: B)

  provides PartialEq[Pair]  :- PartialEq[A], PartialEq[B]
  provides Eq[Pair]         :- Eq[A], Eq[B]
  provides PartialOrd[Pair] :- PartialOrd[A], PartialOrd[B]
  -- WI-1109: TWO conditional provisions, not one. A lexicographic `Pair` is a WEAK
  -- order iff both components are, and a STRONG one iff both are strong — strictly
  -- different conditions, which is exactly why 058 §3.8 puts a `:- goals` tail on the
  -- PROVISION and not on the sort. Same shape as the `PartialEq`/`Eq` pair above.
  provides WeakOrd[Pair] :- WeakOrd[A], WeakOrd[B]
  provides Ord[Pair]    :- Ord[A], Ord[B]

  -- Lexicographic `fst`-then-`snd`. The component compares go through the SEMANTIC
  -- spec op `WeakOrd.compare`, resolving through the provision's condition
  -- slots — so a pair of pairs orders recursively. The tie test is `PartialEq.eq` on
  -- the `Int64` result and not an unqualified `eq`, which inside this sort would be
  -- `Pair.eq` calling itself.
  operation compare(a: Pair, b: Pair) -> Int64 =
    match a
      case pair(al, ar) ->
        match b
          case pair(bl, br) ->
            let c = WeakOrd.compare(al, bl)
            if PartialEq.eq(c, 0) then WeakOrd.compare(ar, br) else c

  operation fst(p: Pair) -> A
  operation snd(p: Pair) -> B
  -- TODO (WI-324 follow-up): add operation bodies. Both `match p case pair(a, _) -> a`
  -- and `p.fst` currently fail the typer with "expected A, got Term" — the entity-field
  -- sort doesn't propagate through self-typed match/access in concrete enum sorts. The
  -- rules below remain as the de-facto implementation until the typer issue is fixed.
  rule fst(pair(?a, ?b)) <=> ?a
  rule snd(pair(?a, ?b)) <=> ?b

  -- Component compares go through the SEMANTIC spec op `PartialEq.eq`, not through
  -- structural unification, so equality dispatches all the way down — a pair of `Set`s
  -- compares its component by MEMBERSHIP. Each call resolves through the matching
  -- slot of the `PartialEq` provision's own condition list above.
  operation eq(a: Pair, b: Pair) -> Bool =
    match a
      case pair(al, ar) ->
        match b
          case pair(bl, br) ->
            if PartialEq.eq(al, bl) then PartialEq.eq(ar, br) else false
end
